<!-- §01 ─ The Hidden Knob ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Hidden Degree of Freedom</div>
<h2>There's a knob nobody optimizes</h2>
<p>
  Autoregressive language models are locked: left-to-right, one token per step, no going back. Masked diffusion models (MDMs) break this constraint — they generate by iteratively revealing tokens from a fully masked sequence, in any order they choose. That flexibility is the whole point.
</p>
<p>
  But here's what almost everyone misses: the <em>order</em> in which tokens are unmasked is a free variable. And it matters enormously. The same pretrained model with the same weights, decoding the same sequence, produces dramatically different results depending on which token it decides to reveal first.
</p>
<p>
  The dominant approach is to use heuristics — pick the most confident token, or the one with the smallest entropy, or just go left-to-right. These are fast and reasonable. They're also myopic. Each step optimizes locally without asking: "does revealing this token now make the remaining reveals easier for the model?" The answer is often no.
</p>

<div class="contrast-row">
  <div class="contrast-panel bad">
    <div class="contrast-head">✗ Myopic (Confidence)</div>
    <canvas id="canvas-myopic" width="240" height="240" style="width:100%;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:#f87171;margin:.5rem 0 0;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">Picks "easy" cells → contradictions emerge → 82.0% puzzle accuracy</p>
  </div>
  <div class="contrast-panel good">
    <div class="contrast-head">✓ Strategic (SAS)</div>
    <canvas id="canvas-sas" width="240" height="240" style="width:100%;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--green);margin:.5rem 0 0;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">Propagates constraints first → clean completion → 91.8% puzzle accuracy</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  What's particularly striking: the human expert ordering — the logical step-by-step strategy a Sudoku master would use — scores only 76.6%, well below even the naïve confidence schedule at 82%. The optimal order for a <em>pretrained diffusion model</em> is not the order that's optimal for human logic. It's shaped by the model's own uncertainty landscape, and only the model can tell you what that is.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 ─ Background ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-background" data-code="background">
<div class="section-no">02 — How Masked Diffusion Models Decode</div>
<h2>Unmasking as a sequential decision</h2>
<p>
  A masked diffusion model starts with a fully masked target sequence and iteratively reveals tokens, one step at a time. At each step, it can look at what's already been revealed and score every remaining masked position. The decoding schedule decides: <em>which position to unmask next?</em>
</p>
<p>
  Formally, let <code>x̃(t)</code> be the partially revealed sequence at step <em>t</em>, with some positions holding ground-truth tokens and others still <code>[MASK]</code>. The model provides a distribution <code>p_θ(xᵢ | x̃(t))</code> over each masked position <em>i</em>. The schedule policy <code>v_ϕ</code> selects which <em>i</em> to unmask next.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">decode-process — sequential unmasking walkthrough</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Next to step through</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="decode-steps">
      <div class="step-box active">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Start: fully masked sequence</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Target: "2 + 3 = 5 total fruits"</div>
          <div class="step-code">[MASK] [MASK] [MASK] [MASK] [MASK] [MASK] [MASK]</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Score: model evaluates each masked position</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The frozen denoiser assigns a confidence distribution p_θ(xᵢ | x̃) over every masked slot. High confidence = the model is sure what goes there.</div>
          <div class="step-code">p("5" | [M][M][M][M][M][M][M]) = 0.02  ← uncertain
p("2" | [M][M][M][M][M][M][M]) = 0.88  ← very confident</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Select: policy chooses which slot to reveal</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The order policy v_ϕ outputs a distribution over all masked positions and selects one. SAS picks the slot that will most benefit <em>future</em> steps.</div>
          <div class="step-code">v_ϕ(i=6 | x̃) = 0.74   ← "total" is foundational
v_ϕ(i=3 | x̃) = 0.08   ← "=" less informative now</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Reveal: sample and update</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The token at the selected position is sampled from the model distribution and the sequence is updated. The dense reward = log p_θ(x_i | x̃) for this step.</div>
          <div class="step-code">[MASK] [MASK] [MASK] [MASK] [MASK] [MASK] "fruits"
reward_t = log p("fruits" | [M][M][M][M][M][M][M])</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">5</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Repeat until complete</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Steps 2–4 repeat n times (sequence length). The cumulative reward R = Σ reward_t over all steps measures how well the model explained the data along this specific order.</div>
          <div class="step-code">final: "2" "+" "3" "=" "5" "total" "fruits"
R(x, σ) = r₁ + r₂ + r₃ + r₄ + r₅ + r₆ + r₇</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="decode-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <button id="decode-next">Next →</button>
      <span class="step-prog" id="decode-prog">Step 1 of 5</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The order σ = (i₁, i₂, …, iₙ) is a permutation of all masked positions. Different orderings trace completely different conditional trajectories through the model's probability space. The decoding distribution induced by a particular schedule can be written as the expectation of the model's pathwise likelihood over all orderings weighted by the policy.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 ─ Theory ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-theory" data-code="theory">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Self-Aware Reward</div>
<h2>The model's own log-likelihood as reward</h2>
<p>
  The insight at the heart of SAS: you don't need an external reward signal (like a correct/incorrect verifier). The frozen diffusion model itself provides a dense reward — its own pathwise log-likelihood along the decoding trajectory.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Self-Aware Reward</div>
  <span class="math-var">R</span><sub>θ</sub>(<span class="math-var">x</span>, <span class="math-var">σ</span>) = log <span class="math-var">p</span><sub>θ</sub>(<span class="math-var">x</span> | <span class="math-var">σ</span>) = <span class="math-var">Σ</span><sub>t=1..n</sub> log <span class="math-var">p</span><sub>θ</sub><sup><span class="math-var">iₜ</span></sup>(<span class="math-var">x</span><sub>iₜ</sub> | <span class="math-var">x̃</span><sup>(t-1)</sup>(<span class="math-var">σ</span>))
  <br><span class="math-comment">= sum of log-probabilities of each revealed token, given what the model has seen so far</span>
</div>

<p>
  A higher reward means the model was more confident at each reveal step — fewer surprises, easier predictions. A good schedule <em>makes the model's job easier</em> at every step by strategically choosing which information to reveal first.
</p>

<p>
  The theoretical grounding (Theorem 3.1) shows this reward is a lower bound on the log marginal likelihood: maximizing it maximizes a ELBO on how well the induced decoding distribution matches the data distribution. Theorem 3.2 goes further: minimizing the self-aware loss is equivalent (up to data entropy) to minimizing the joint KL between data-policy and model-policy distributions — a principled objective for order selection.
</p>

