<p>
  The key design insight: the policy head is <em>lightweight</em> — a small MLP or one-layer Transformer on top of the frozen model's intermediate features. Training it costs a fraction of full RL on the denoiser. At inference, the policy adds negligible overhead: one extra forward pass per decode step to score positions.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §04 ─ Algorithm ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-algorithm" data-code="algorithm">
<div class="section-no">04 — SAS: Training the Order Policy</div>
<h2>GRPO for order optimization</h2>
<p>
  Order learning is cast as a finite-horizon MDP: the state is the partially-revealed sequence, the action is which position to unmask next, and the reward is the frozen model's log-probability of the revealed token. SAS trains this policy with Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) — a critic-free RL method that uses within-group reward normalization to estimate advantages.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">grpo-training — SAS policy optimization loop</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Next to step through</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="grpo-steps">
      <div class="step-box active">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Sample: draw G order sequences from current policy</div>
          <div class="step-desc">For each training sequence x, sample G different decode orders σ⁽¹⁾…σ⁽ᴳ⁾ from the current policy v_ϕ. No value function needed — GRPO is critic-free.</div>
          <div class="step-code">orders = [policy.sample(x) for _ in range(G)]  # G=8 typically</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Evaluate: frozen denoiser scores each trajectory</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Run teacher-forcing along each sampled order: reveal tokens from x in the sampled order and compute the frozen model's log-likelihood at each step. This is pure inference — the denoiser weights don't move.</div>
          <div class="step-code">R[i] = sum(log denoiser(x[iₜ] | x̃ᵗ⁻¹(σ⁽ⁱ⁾)) for t in range(n))</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Normalize: group-relative advantage estimation</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Within the group of G trajectories, standardize rewards to get advantages. High reward = the order made the model's predictions accurate → positive advantage.</div>
          <div class="step-code">A_hat[i] = (R[i] - mean(R)) / (std(R) + eps)  # per-group</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Update: maximize clipped surrogate objective</div>
          <div class="step-desc">GRPO clips the probability ratio to prevent large policy updates. The gradient flows only through the policy head parameters ϕ — the frozen denoiser θ receives no gradients.</div>
          <div class="step-code">loss = -mean(min(ratio * A, clip(ratio, 1-ε, 1+ε) * A))
# ratio = v_ϕ(σₜ|sₜ₋₁) / v_ϕ_old(σₜ|sₜ₋₁)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">5</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Repeat: policy converges, denoiser stays frozen</div>
          <div class="step-desc">After convergence, the order policy is applied at inference time. For additional gains, a second-stage fine-tunes the denoiser weights θ along the learned fixed order — but this is optional.</div>
          <div class="step-code"># Stage 2 (optional): fine-tune θ under fixed learned order σ̂
loss_sft = -mean(log denoiser(x[iₜ] | x̃ᵗ⁻¹(σ̂)) for t in 1..n)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="grpo-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <button id="grpo-next">Next →</button>
      <span class="step-prog" id="grpo-prog">Step 1 of 5</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Dense vs. sparse reward</h3>
<p>
  A natural alternative is RLVR-style training with a sparse 0/1 terminal reward (was the final answer correct?). The paper compares both on GSM8K: the dense self-aware reward consistently matches or outperforms the sparse reward across generation lengths and block sizes. The advantage: dense rewards assign credit to each individual reveal decision, while sparse rewards leave all intermediate steps ambiguous.
</p>
</section>

