<h3>What the learned schedule discovers</h3>
<p>
  SAS learns an "easy-to-hard curriculum" that mirrors Sudoku solving logic — but driven by the model's uncertainty, not human rules. The policy prioritizes <strong>naked singles</strong> first: blank cells where the current context leaves only one valid digit. Once those are filled, the propagated constraints reveal the next tier of solvable cells, and so on. The heatmap shows clear spatial clustering: easy cells (light) unlock a wave of medium-difficulty cells (medium blue), which unlock the hardest ones last (dark purple).
</p>
<p>
  The human expert order applies a similar logic, but calibrated to human solving strategies. Crucially, "what's easy for a human" and "what's easy for the model" are different. A Sudoku expert's logic follows crisp combinatorial rules; the pretrained diffusion model's uncertainty is shaped by training data distributions and token co-occurrence statistics. SAS adapts to the model, not the other way around.
</p>
</section>

